        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# 04 \u00b7 control/style-transfer\n\nTwo images in: a **composition** to keep and a **look** to apply. The only endpoint of the four with more than one dial, and the closest thing in the Stability API to what you would do locally with IPAdapter.\n\n## The two inputs\n\n- `init_image` \u2014 the newest file in `directory`. This is the composition being preserved.\n- `style_image` \u2014 the newest file in `stability/style-refs/`. This is the look being applied.\n\nBoth are required. Getting them the wrong way round produces a plausible-looking image that is exactly backwards, and it is an easy mistake to make because neither slot complains.\n\n## The three dials\n\nThis graph sweeps `composition_fidelity`, because it is the one you will actually reach for. The other two are pinned; move them once you can see what this one does.\n\n- **`composition_fidelity`** (swept: 0.5 / 0.8 / 0.95) \u2014 how hard the init image's arrangement is held. **This is the dial to raise when structure drifts.** At 0.5 the composition is a starting point; at 0.95 it barely moves.\n- **`style_strength`** (pinned 1.0) \u2014 how much of the reference's look lands. Lower it when the style overwhelms the subject.\n- **`change_strength`** (pinned 0.9) \u2014 how far from the init image the result may travel *at all*, before either of the other two apply. Think of it as the outer bound. Drop it to ~0.5 for a restrained pass.\n\nThey interact: high `composition_fidelity` with low `change_strength` gives you something very close to the original, and there is a point past which you are paying for a copy.\n\n## Where this sits against the other three\n\nStructure holds arrangement but takes its look from the prompt. This holds arrangement and takes its look from a *picture*. When you have an approved card whose style you want spread across the deck, this is the endpoint \u2014 and it is what the deck cascade's anchor plates are ultimately for.\n\n## Chaining these four\n\nThey all share one convention, and that is the whole mechanism \u2014 there is no workflow-calls-a-workflow in ComfyUI, so a folder is the join:\n\n```\nstability/in/              drop your sources here\nstability/out/sketch/      <- 01 writes here\nstability/out/structure/   <- 02\nstability/out/style/       <- 03\nstability/out/transfer/    <- 04\n```\n\nEvery one of these graphs reads **the newest image** in its `directory` (`sort_by = modified`, `descending`, `limit = 1`). So to feed one endpoint's output into another, change this graph's `directory` to that graph's output folder and run it. Structure reading `stability/out/sketch` is a two-step chain; nothing else needs rewiring.\n\nThe filename carries through: `Load Images From Directory` emits `filenames`, which is wired to the save node's `label`, so `the-fool.png` stays `the-fool.png` at every stage and you can always tell which source a result came from. `overwrite` is off, so a second run lands as `the-fool_1.png` rather than destroying the first \u2014 and \"newest by modified\" still picks it up.\n\nEach result gets a sidecar JSON with the prompt and settings that made it.\n\n## Cost\n\nEach of the three nodes below is **one paid call**, so a run is three. That is the point of a study graph \u2014 you are buying the comparison, not the image. **Ctrl+B** any of them to drop it from the run once you know which end of the dial you care about; bypass passes the input straight through and costs nothing.\n\nSeeds are fixed at **7**. Stability treats seed 0 as \"pick a random one\", which would make the three results incomparable and stop any of them being cached. With the seed pinned, the dial is the only thing that differs between them.\n\n## The prompt lives in one node\n\nThe **Shared Prompt** node feeds every `prompt` field in this graph \u2014 all three settings \u2014 and their `negative` too, so there is one\nplace to edit and the copies cannot drift apart. The prompt ships empty here on purpose: with both images supplied, the endpoint does better with nothing to argue against. The `negative` is still shared.\n\nIt emits a plain string, **not** a list. That is the difference between it and **Prompt\nList**, which would make each node it feeds run once per line and multiply what the graph\ncosts."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 control/style-transfer",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
